    "slot_value": "Must support interoperability and sharing across compatible applications; platform limitations that affect sharing, backup, or interoperability are a serious concern. If a client can’t support everything, it should still work safely with the subset it understands, using graceful degradation: limited clients can read and update only what they support without corrupting or dropping shared information, breaking the overall structure, or hiding unavailable features. Shared changes and backups should preserve authoritative data independent of any one application, and any feature that can’t be represented in a given client should be preserved as data or metadata rather than lost. A partially compatible client should be allowed to read and make only the kinds of changes it fully understands, and ZNIX should block or isolate anything ambiguous. Unknown fields, relationships, or object types must be preserved untouched and not overwritten when the record is saved back. Updates should be merged so they do not discard information added by another client. If safe handling cannot be guaranteed, the client should be limited to read-only or a narrower edit mode.",
